          "GraphsPerPage": 6,
          "Name": "New Zealand",
          "ResourceName": null,
          "Children": [
            {
              "$type": "Models.Factorial.Experiment, Models",
              "DisabledSimNames": null,
              "Name": "RS_Oats_9899",
              "ResourceName": null,
              "Children": [
                {
                  "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
                  "Text": "Data from:\r\n\r\n[martin2001effect]\r\n\r\n\tThe response of oats (Avena sativa L.) to timing and intensity of drought was determined in a mobile\r\nrainshelter, which excluded rainfall during crop growth. Ten irrigation treatments subjected the crops to\r\ndrought of varying duration at different stages during plant growth; the crops were otherwise fully\r\nirrigated. Oat grain yield generally increased linearly with cumulative water use, and decreased linearly as\r\nthe maximum potential soil moisture deficit experienced during crop growth increased, regardless of the\r\ntiming of drought. The exception to this was the no drought (fully irrigated) treatment, where lodging\r\nreduced panicle number and grain weight compared to early drought treatments. Drought affected yield\r\nmainly by reducing panicle and grain number, effects on grain weight were small. . Relieving early drought\r\nthrough late irrigation led to more late tillers, more panicles, heavier grains, and later maturity. Full and\r\nlate drought increased screenings.\r\n\r\n",
                  "Name": "Experiment Description",
                  "ResourceName": null,
                  "Children": [],
                  "Enabled": true,
                  "ReadOnly": false
                },
